For C, continue with the code from Lab 3

to compile with clang

clang -g -pthread concurrent.c -o concurrent

to run with valgrind/helgrind:

valgrind --tool=helgrind ./preflow < ../../data

Best test result:

real 0m11.479s
user 0m11.065s
sys 0m11.417s

Measurement for 1. without Atomic variable

real 0m8.168s
user 0m7.416s
sys 0m0.184s

Measurment for 1. with atomic variable

real 0m9.572s
user 0m7.032s
sys 0m0.195s

### Assembly code with Atomic variable.

relabel:

mov DWORD PTR hello[rip], eax
mov DWORD PTR -16[rbp], 1                         # Store 1 in temp var
mov eax, DWORD PTR -16[rbp]                     # Load 1
mov ecx, eax                                     # Copy to exc  
mov rdx, QWORD PTR -32[rbp]                    #
mov eax, ecx
lock xadd DWORD PTR [rdx], eax               # ATOMIC operation here
add eax, ecx                                 # Complete the atomic operation
mov DWORD PTR -12[rbp], eax                  # Store result
mov eax, DWORD PTR hello[rip]
and eax, 22136
mov DWORD PTR hello[rip], eax


### Assembly code without the atomic variable.


mov DWORD PTR hello[rip], eax
mov rax, QWORD PTR -16[rbp]                          # load u pointer
mov eax, DWORD PTR [rax]                             # load u -> h
lea edx, 1[rax] # calculate u -> h 1
mov rax, QWORD PTR -16[rbp]                          # reload u pointer
mov DWORD PTR [rax], edx                             # store u -> h + 1
mov eax, DWORD PTR hello[rip]
and eax, 22136
mov DWORD PTR hello[rip], eax

Conclusion:

The lock instruction is expensive
Non atomic, just copy a value
Atomic, lock xadd - must codinate iwth all CPU cores

The lock forces the CPU to stop, wait, execute one instruction at a time.

Memory/Cache synchronization

Each CPU core can work with its own cache copy
Atomic: must ensure all CPU core see the same value immediately.

-> more work to do for the CPU.
